# Compile-time evaluation.

use std::abilities::embed::{ Embeddable }

## Evaluate `eval` while the program is being compiled, and replace this call
## site with the result.
##
##     use std::comptime::{ comptime }
##
##     let table = comptime { eval = {} -> build_lookup_table {} }
##
## `table` costs nothing at run time: by the time the program runs, the call is
## gone and the value is a literal in the compiled output.
##
## ## What may be returned
##
## Whatever `Embeddable` admits — data all the way down. The result has to
## outlive the compiler process: it is serialized into the program's IR, stored,
## and for `mez build --native` frozen into the image heap, then rebuilt
## elsewhere. A function, a mutable cell, a `Promise`, a foreign object cannot
## make that crossing. See `std::abilities::embed`.
##
## ## Effects
##
## Anything, as long as the thunk handles it itself:
##
##     let config = comptime { eval = {} ->
##         perform RealFileSystem in read_file { path = "app.config" }
##     }
##
## The handlers are the ordinary ones — there is no separate compile-time
## handler set, because the evaluator *is* the interpreter. Which handler you
## perform is part of this block's content hash, so choosing a deterministic one
## is how you get a reproducible build, and choosing a real one is how you read
## the actual filesystem. That is your decision, not the compiler's.
##
## `<*>` on the `eval` slot is the contract, stated rather than implied: every
## effect the body performs is handled by the body. Nothing escapes a comptime
## block, and nothing it did survives into the program's type — the effects
## happened, during compilation, and are erased.
##
## ## When it runs
##
## During `mez build` / `build --native` / `dev` / `exec` — never during
## `mez check` or in the editor. Type checking needs this block's *type*, which
## inference reads off the thunk; it never needs the *value*. So an editor
## never executes your code, and a comptime that loops forever cannot hang it.
##
## The cost is that `check` is weaker than `build`: a comptime that crashes
## passes `check` and fails `build`. `Embeddable` is checked either way.
##
## ## Restrictions
##
## The thunk must be **closed** — it cannot capture anything from an enclosing
## function, because those are run-time values that do not exist yet:
##
##     let scale = { n } ->
##         comptime { eval = {} -> n * 2 }   # rejected: `n` is a run-time value
##
## Top-level functions and values are fine; they are reachable by hash.
##
## `comptime` must also be **called directly**. It is not a first-class value:
## passing it around or partially applying it is rejected, because the whole
## mechanism is that this call site gets replaced.
##
## ## The body below
##
## `eval {}` is a deliberate run-time fallback with the same meaning: if a
## comptime call ever reaches run time — because the reduction pass was
## disabled — the program still computes the right answer, just not for free.
##
## ## The `<*>` on the RESULT
##
## Not decoration: calling `eval` propagates its `performed` marker, so this is
## the row the body actually has. Saying so is what makes compile-time work
## visible to whoever imports you — `*` cannot be annotated away, so a library
## that runs a `comptime` block carries the mark into its own signature and a
## consumer can tell it apart from one that does not.
pub let comptime: { eval: {} -> 'a <*> } with Embeddable 'a -> 'a <*> = { eval } -> eval {}

## Fail the build, from inside a `comptime` block.
##
##     comptime { eval = {} -> match validate { config = cfg } is
##         Ok {} -> cfg
##         Err { error } -> compile_error { msg = "bad config: $(error)" }
##     }
##
## Needed because in Mezze a failure is usually a VALUE: returning
## `Err { … }` from a comptime block embeds the error and compiles fine,
## which is the right default. This is how you say the other thing — that
## this particular `Err` should stop the build — and the message is
## reported at the `comptime` call site.
##
## Outside a comptime block it is an ordinary function that raises, so
## there is no separate "compile-time only" rule to learn.
pub let compile_error: { msg: Str } -> 'a = { msg } ->
  __native_comptime_compile_error { msg = msg }
